  "source": {
    "display_title": "Image-Based Crowd Rendering for Virtual Cities",
    "authors": [
      "Franco Tecchia",
      "Céline Loscos",
      "Yiorgos Chrysanthou"
    ]
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Image-based impostor rendering with positional and directional discretization can display dense animated crowds in large virtual urban environments at interactive rates.",
      "short_title": "Impostors enable real-time crowds",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "To display many different individual people at interactive frame rates, we combined texture compression with multipass rendering.",
        "Our system allows real-time rendering of densely populated large-scale environments. The rendering speed is independent of the avatar model’s complexity."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Perturbing an impostor plane toward a PCA-derived best-fitting plane reduces viewpoint-change popping for walking human impostors while limiting newly introduced distortions.",
      "short_title": "PCA planes reduce popping",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Starting from an impostor plane purely perpendicular to the camera, we “perturbed” its orientation using the resulting plane obtained from a PCA of the sample image. This minimized the popping while limiting the introduction of other artifacts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Packing each prerendered human sample into the smallest containing rectangle reduces the texture memory needed for human images to one quarter of the original value without loss of image quality.",
      "short_title": "Sample packing cuts memory",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Thanks to this process, the resulting new image is much smaller than the original without any loss of image quality.",
        "With our current reorganization strategy, we can reduce the amount of texture memory necessary to store our human images to 25 percent of the original value."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "OpenGL compressed textures and compact frame storage allow ten human types with ten animation frames each to fit in about 25 Mbytes of texture memory.",
      "short_title": "Compressed textures enable variety",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "We then stored the texture using the OpenGL compressed format, which gives a further memory compression ratio of 1:4.",
        "To simulate 10 different types of human with 10 frames of animation each, we need about 25 Mbytes of texture memory."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Alpha-channel multipass rendering can recolor significant body regions such as clothes, hair, and skin to increase avatar variety while preserving baked texture shading.",
      "short_title": "Multipass coloring diversifies avatars",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "We performed multipass rendering using the alpha channel to select the parts to render. For each pass, we change the impostor polygon color to the desired color.",
        "Computing a texture modulation preserves the shading because it’s already included in the texture."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A regular grid of environment tiles can support both collision behavior and shadowing decisions for moving virtual humans.",
      "short_title": "Grid tiles guide motion",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "To control the virtual humans’ motion, we subdivided the floor of the environment into regular-sized tiles.",
        "Several types of information can be stored, but presently, we only use this information for collision detection and shadowing."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The same image database used for impostors can project posture-consistent human shadows onto the ground at the cost of one additional polygon.",
      "short_title": "Impostors project human shadows",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Using the impostor approach, we can also compute and display the moving humans’ shadows.",
        "With the cost of only one additional polygon corresponding to the projection of the impostor polygon on the ground, we create a shadow corresponding to the human posture."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The tested system renders 10,000 different humans in a 41,260-polygon village at 12 to 20 frames per second, with measured frame rates falling from 26.20 fps at zero people to 13.35 fps at 10,000 people.",
      "short_title": "Ten thousand humans remain interactive",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "These humans move in a village modeled with 41,260 polygons. The display updates between 12 and 20 frames per second, depending on the displayed polygonal complexity.",
        "The frame per second rate for 0 people was 26.20; for 1,000 people, it was 24.08; for 5,000 people, it was 18.26; and for 10,000 people, it was 13.35."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-pca-impostor-plane--3f1a9c0d2b4e",
      "title": "PCA-Perturbed Impostor Plane",
      "excerpt": "To apply this idea, we had to project back in the 3D space the points visible in the impostor image to get 3D visible samples of the object. We applied a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to the set of 3D points and identified the two principal eigenvectors as directions describing the projection plane. Starting from an impostor plane purely perpendicular to the camera, we “perturbed” its orientation using the resulting plane obtained from a PCA of the sample image. This minimized the popping while limiting the introduction of other artifacts.",
      "overview": "This formal argument explains why the impostor plane is not chosen mechanically from the original camera direction. By fitting visible 3D sample points with PCA and then averaging that orientation with the conventional perpendicular plane, the method targets the dominant popping artifact without accepting the full distortion of the best-fitting plane alone.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "1"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.39",
        "Fig.2",
        "Fig.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-packed-texture-memory--6c9d2e4f8a10",
      "title": "Packed Sample Memory Reduction",
      "excerpt": "During the preprocessing phase, we computed the smallest rectangle containing the character for each sample. Then, we combined all these samples in a single image, reorganizing them to minimize the unused space. Thanks to this process, the resulting new image is much smaller than the original without any loss of image quality. With our current reorganization strategy, we can reduce the amount of texture memory necessary to store our human images to 25 percent of the original value.",
      "overview": "The memory reduction is a concrete measurement tied to the paper's texture layout change. It supports the claim that the system gains crowd variety by removing empty regions around prerendered avatars rather than by reducing image fidelity, although the result depends on preprocessing sample rectangles for each impostor frame.",
      "evidence_type": "measurement",
      "claim_refs": [
        "2",
        "3"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.40",
        "Fig.5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-s3tc-avatar-budget--7ab42c91de03",
      "title": "Compressed Avatar Texture Budget",
      "excerpt": "We then stored the texture using the OpenGL compressed format, which gives a further memory compression ratio of 1:4. The compression format lets us keep alpha values and encodes them in 4 bits. Once loaded in texture memory, each animation frame for a single human model requires 256 Kbytes. To simulate 10 different types of human with 10 frames of animation each, we need about 25 Mbytes of texture memory.",
      "overview": "These numeric constraints show the system's practical texture budget after compression. They connect frame-level storage, alpha-channel preservation, and total memory for a multi-avatar crowd, while also indicating a quality trade-off because the cited compression loses some image quality.",
      "evidence_type": "measurement",
      "claim_refs": [
        "3",
        "4"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.40"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-alpha-multipass-colors--0f12ab34cd56",
      "title": "Alpha-Guided Multipass Coloring",
      "excerpt": "To identify such areas, we precomputed an alpha-channel image with a different alpha value for each part to modify. Tuning the alpha channel, we can define up to 256 different regions in the texture without using compression, or up to 16 using the s3tc compression because only 4 bits are available for the alpha channel. We performed multipass rendering using the alpha channel to select the parts to render. For each pass, we change the impostor polygon color to the desired color, and we apply the texture using the flag GL_MODULATE.",
      "overview": "This model constraint describes how a single rendered human asset can produce multiple appearances. The alpha channel partitions the avatar into recolorable regions and GL_MODULATE changes selected regions per pass, but the method has an explicit trade-off: more passes improve variety at the cost of rendering time.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
      "claim_refs": [
        "4"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.40",
        "Fig.6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-grid-shadow-collision--a41b2c3d4e5f",
      "title": "Grid-Based Motion and Shadows",
      "excerpt": "To control the virtual humans’ motion, we subdivided the floor of the environment into regular-sized tiles. While the humans move around, they check information corresponding to the tile they occupy. Several types of information can be stored, but presently, we only use this information for collision detection and shadowing. When we encode the tile with black, it’s impassable and the avatar needs to change direction. In addition, we performed intercollision detection between humans by checking if a destination tile is already occupied.",
      "overview": "The grid evidence matters because the rendering system also needs crowd behavior and spatial constraints. It establishes that motion decisions are not solved by the impostor images alone; a tile map supplies collision and shadow state, including impassable cells and checks for occupied destinations.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
      "claim_refs": [
        "5"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.41"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-impostor-shadow-polygon--5e4d3c2b1a09",
      "title": "One-Polygon Human Shadows",
      "excerpt": "Using the impostor approach, we can also compute and display the moving humans’ shadows. To project the humans’ shadows on the ground, we exploit the fact that the shadow of a virtual human is the projection onto the ground of its silhouette as seen from the light source. We can thus display the humans’ shadow on the ground using one of the images already stored in the database that we use for the impostor. In this way, with the cost of only one additional polygon corresponding to the projection of the impostor polygon on the ground, we create a shadow corresponding to the human posture.",
      "overview": "This figure-level result extends the impostor database beyond avatar appearance. It shows that the same stored silhouettes can create posture-aware ground shadows cheaply, which supports the paper's claim that visual quality can improve without returning to fully polygonal animated crowds.",
      "evidence_type": "figure_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "6"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.41",
        "Fig.7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-ten-thousand-results--9c8b7a6d5e4f",
      "title": "Ten-Thousand Avatar Benchmark",
      "excerpt": "For the results in Figures 8 and 9, we displayed 2,000 avatars for each of four types of humans and 1,000 for each of the two last type, thus displaying 10,000 different humans. One of these types is a jogger, thus having an animation different from the others. These humans move in a village modeled with 41,260 polygons. The display updates between 12 and 20 frames per second, depending on the displayed polygonal complexity. To evaluate our simulation’s scalability, we simulated different sized crowds. The frame per second rate for 0 people was 26.20; for 1,000 people, it was 24.08; for 5,000 people, it was 18.26; and for 10,000 people, it was 13.35.",
      "overview": "The benchmark is the clearest empirical evidence for the rendering pipeline. It gives the hardware-independent reader concrete crowd sizes, scene complexity, and frame-rate measurements, while also showing that the baseline city model itself consumes substantial rendering resources.",
      "evidence_type": "measurement",
      "claim_refs": [
        "0",
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-image-based-crowd-rendering--565ae2b0e3a1",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.41-42",
        "Fig.8",
        "Fig.9",
        "Fig.10"
      ]
    }
  ],
